r at the  expense of common people this was  expressed by the founders deciding to  separate from Britain and establish a  government meant to empower common  people not just a powerful overclass the  creation of America as it took place was  in and of itself an extraordinarily  progressive act progressivism was  expressed in the founders agreement to  abolish  the first constitution of America called  the Articles of Confederation an update  and reinvent it into something that they  deemed was sort of more useful in a new  era instead of treating those Articles  of Confederation as something divine and  eternally perfect we should apply the  same logic to the Constitution today not  to mention that they replace the  Articles of Confederation with something  far more progressive Constitution that  in contrast to those first Articles of  Confederation provided a strong central  government and a court system federal  taxes federal trade regulations  legislative representation based on  population loser requirements for  adopting new laws in the future and  amending the Constitution so not only  were our founders progressive in  reimagining our country's fundamental  framework into something more practical  they actually reimagined it into  something that was far to the left of  the decentralized libertarian mess that  existed before but now let's circle back  to the very beginning after everything  I've told you about the founding fathers  being progressive we actually shouldn't  care so much about the intent of  politicians from the 18th century anyway  because it's 2019 when conservatives  today tell us that the text of the  Constitution and the intent of the  framers are all we need to deal with  today's problems in a society that would  be unrecognizable to someone that lived  200 years ago it doesn't make any sense  whatsoever but as long as conservatives  attempt to claim the founders as being  exclusively a part of their own  ideological tradition I'd like to p